== The Reasons

The point being proved in this way by #emph[Scriptures] and #emph[examples,] we come now to the reasons, for its further confirmation.

1\. Reason. #emph[It may be taken from the goodness and bounty of God,] which is such that he will not allow any work of charity shown to any of his ministers or children to pass away unrewarded, without a full recompense. So #emph[David] says in Ps 62:12, “To You, O Lord, belongs mercy. For You render to each man according to his work.” Though God does not render a reward to any man #emph[for his work done,] yet he does render to every man #emph[according to his work.] Indeed, God always rewards beyond measure, to give evidence of his grace and bounty. God will not be in any man’s debt for long; what a man has paid out on God’s account, he will speedily return into his bosom with advantage.

2\. Reason. #emph[It may be taken from the faithfulness and righteousness of God.] He has graciously promised in his word to recompense and reward our kindness abundantly, and his faithfulness and righteousness bind him to make good what he has promised, so that he cannot help but be as good as his word. Men may be quick to promise but slow to perform. But with God, who is the true and faithful one, #emph[dictum et factum,] saying and doing, are one and the same: #emph[all his promises are Yes and Amen in Christ Jesus.] The apostle says in Heb 6:10, “God is not unrighteous. He will not forget your#super[+] work and labor of love which you have shown for His name as you have ministered to the saints and continue to do so.” Here the apostle argues that a reward is certain for those who minister anything to the saints, from the #emph[righteousness of God:] because he is righteous, he will not fail to do what he has promised. He is not unrighteous so as to FORGET; that is, he will righteously remember. And God’s remembering means the same as his recompensing. As he will remember sinners by repaying their evil ways on their own heads, so he will remember his saints by returning the good they have done into their own bosoms.

To assure us all the more of this kind of remembrance from God, the Holy Spirit mentions certain #emph[books,] or #emph[scrolls of remembrance written before God,] in which the merciful deeds of his servants are recorded. So it is no more possible that those who are charitably kind and helpful to the poor ministers and people of God should lose their reward, than that God himself should cease to be righteous, or forget his word.

3\. Reason. #emph[It may be taken from several metaphorical expressions used in Scripture, by which almsgiving is represented to us,] all of which imply not only a certain return but a return with increase, such as #emph[SOWING,] and #emph[LENDING,] and that at usury.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[We find it set out in Scripture by] SOWING, 2 Cor 9:6: “Whoever sows sparingly will also reap sparingly, and whoever sows bountifully will also reap bountifully.” The apostle applies this proverbial saying to the giving out of alms. Now #emph[farmers] who sow their grain with a liberal hand usually reap a crop to match. In the same way, Christians who sow their seed of charity with an open, plentiful hand will reap a plentiful crop to match. They will find that the seed they have sown comes up with increase, yielding #emph[thirty,] if not #emph[sixty,] or #emph[a hundredfold here,] besides eternal life hereafter. It may be that you will not reap the fruit of your seed at once; and what wonder is that? Who is there that sows and expects to reap the same day? The farmer waits for his harvest. Wait on the Lord, and do not doubt that a harvest will come that will pay you both for your sowing and for your waiting.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The second #emph[metaphor] by which almsgiving is set out in Scripture is #emph[LENDING,] and lending at interest. Prov 19:17: “Whoever is kind to the poor lends to the #smallcaps[Lord], and He will repay him for what he has given.” Men who lend to men receive their own back with increase. What they lay up lies dead, and the thief may perhaps break in and steal it; but what they lend, if it is to safe hands, comes back with advantage. How rich some usurers grow by this trade of lending! And though it is a #emph[paradox] that #emph[giving] is a richer trade than #emph[lending,] even at interest, yet it is a certain truth. For #emph[this giving] is lending, and the man who lends to the Lord will find a greater income at the year’s end than the man who lends to the best of men. So, as one well says, #emph[Eleemosyna non est divitiarum dispendium, sed ditescendi potius compendium, quaestusque; omnium uberrimus:] giving to the poor is not the way to waste our wealth, but the art of thriving, and the shortest road to riches. Though our alms-deeds seem (like the seed sown) to perish and rot, yet if we believe what we do not see, we will surely see what we believe. We will find, even here, such a fruitful increase that we will conclude #emph[that giving to charitable uses is the surest and safest way of getting and thriving in the world.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So you#super[+] have the truth of the point confirmed by #emph[Scriptures, examples,] and #emph[reasons.] We come now to the application.]]
